  "text": {
    "intro": "The Tuesday practice, and a tower captain keeping a ledger of everyone's mistakes.\nFive ringers are involved. Determine each ringer's round, bell and slip.\n- **Ringer**: Fenwick, Lavinia, Dorcas, Ambrose and Prudence.\n- **Round** (listed from earlier to later): round one, round two, round three, round four and round five.\n- **Bell**: the fifth, the fourth, the tenor, the sharp second and the third.\n- **Slip**: a fired shot, a missed dodge, a dropped rope, nothing at all and a clipped backstroke.\nEvery ringer has a different round, bell and slip, so within each category every item is used exactly once.",
    "clues": [
      "Round two is exactly one round earlier than Dorcas, with no round in between. The ringer who takes the tenor is later than round two in round order.",
      "The ringer who is blamed for a missed dodge and round two are exactly one round apart in round order, in one direction or the other.",
      "The ringer who is blamed for nothing at all and round four are exactly one round apart in round order, in one direction or the other.",
      "Ambrose does not ring in round one. The ringer who takes the tenor falls somewhere between Ambrose and Fenwick in round order.",
      "At least one of the following holds, and possibly more than one: (a) The ringer who takes the tenor is later than round four in round order. (b) The ringer who takes the fifth and Fenwick both sit at odd-numbered rounds or both at even-numbered rounds.",
      "The ringer who takes the fourth and the ringer who is blamed for a clipped backstroke are exactly one round apart in round order, in one direction or the other.",
      "Lavinia falls somewhere between the ringer who is blamed for a clipped backstroke and the ringer who is blamed for a dropped rope in round order.",
      "The ringer who takes the tenor and the ringer who is blamed for a dropped rope are exactly three rounds apart in round order.",
      "The ringer who is blamed for a clipped backstroke is exactly one round earlier than the ringer who takes the third, with no round in between."
    ]
  }
